# Citation metadata for the Tractor Beam Cathedral project.
# Format: https://citation-file-format.github.io/  (CFF 1.2.0)
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AI ASSISTANCE DISCLOSURE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This project was developed with extensive AI assistance, and says so here
# because the disclosure is part of the work rather than a footnote to it: the
# README states an explicit, falsifiable hypothesis about whether AI tooling can
# let an educated amateur produce results consistent with the literature, and
# the intended paper is partly a case study in how much expert help that takes.
# A project claiming that cannot leave its own provenance vague.
#
# As of commit 93f215c (2026-08-06), 61 commits since 2026-07-26:
#
#     Claude Opus 5     35 commits    Co-Authored-By trailer
#     Claude Sonnet 5   20 commits    Co-Authored-By trailer
#     ------------------------------------------------------
#     with AI trailer   55 of 61      (6 commits human-only)
#
# Those counts are a SNAPSHOT, not a live figure. The authoritative record is
# the `Co-Authored-By:` trailers in the git history itself:
#
#     git log --format='%b' | grep -i '^Co-Authored-By:' | sort | uniq -c
#
# The models are NOT listed as authors, and must not be. Nature, Science and
# all Springer-Nature journals hold that Large Language Models do not satisfy
# authorship criteria, because authorship carries accountability that cannot be
# assigned to a tool. Disclosure is required; authorship is prohibited. That is
# an industry-wide consensus, not a Nature idiosyncrasy.
#
# Accountability for every claim in this repository rests with the human author
# below -- including the seven documentation-versus-code defects the project
# found in its own records, which are logged in docs/CLAIMS.md rather than
# quietly corrected.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this software or its results, please cite it using the metadata
  below. Note the feasibility ledger: this framework quantifies its own distance
  from the application that motivates it, and any citation of its results should
  carry that distance with them.

title: "Tractor Beam Cathedral (gwtb): a spin-2 phased-array framework for engineered gravitational radiation"
abstract: >-
  A modeling and simulation framework for a theoretical asteroid-deflection
  concept: arrays of massive spheres undergoing controlled finite maneuvers,
  phased so their gravitational radiation constructively interferes at a target
  at 40 AU. Its transferable contribution is the extension of phased-array
  theory from spin-1 (electromagnetic) to spin-2 (gravitational) fields --
  element mismatch is cos(2*dpsi) rather than cos(dpsi), elements 90 degrees
  apart cancel completely where electromagnetic intuition predicts a doubling of
  power, and the orientation tolerance for 1% gain loss is exactly twice as
  tight. Because no external reference implementation exists to check such
  results against, the framework enforces its own auditability: every physics
  function carries a machine-checked citation to a numbered equation in an
  open-access source, claims are registered as established, derived or
  conjectural, every approximation records the regime in which it fails, and
  results computed from momentum-non-conserving sources are inseparable from an
  UNPHYSICAL provenance stamp. A feasibility ledger is emitted on every run
  stating the quantitative gap between the modeled configuration and an actual
  deflection.

type: software
license: Apache-2.0
version: 0.1.1
date-released: "2026-08-16"
repository-code: "https://github.com/sudo-install-gravity/tractor-beam-cathedral"
url: "https://github.com/sudo-install-gravity/tractor-beam-cathedral"

# Zenodo's "concept DOI" -- always resolves to the latest archived version,
# rather than pinning to v0.1.1 specifically. The first archive was minted from
# the v0.1.1 GitHub release (the first release created after the GitHub-Zenodo
# integration was enabled; Zenodo does not backfill releases published before
# that, hence v0.1.0 itself has no archive). v0.1.1's own version-specific DOI
# is 10.5281/zenodo.21960957 -- correct to cite this exact snapshot, but do not
# swap it in here, since this field should stay evergreen across releases.
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: "10.5281/zenodo.21960956"
    description: "Zenodo archive (concept DOI -- always resolves to the latest version)"

authors:
  - given-names: Paul
    family-names: Day
    alias: sudo-install-gravity
    email: dpaulday@protonmail.com
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0007-1522-7282"

contact:
  - given-names: Paul
    family-names: Day
    email: dpaulday@protonmail.com

keywords:
  - gravitational waves
  - linearized gravity
  - spin-2 fields
  - phased arrays
  - asteroid deflection
  - planetary defence
  - research software engineering
  - computational reproducibility
  - scientific provenance

# The three sources the framework's spine rests on. The complete registry --
# 54 equations, each mapped to a source, an implementation and a test -- is in
# docs/INDEX.md section 1, and docs/ERRATA.md records verified errors found in
# the published literature.
references:
  - type: article
    title: >-
      Gravitational radiation from post-Newtonian sources and inspiralling
      compact binaries
    authors:
      - given-names: Luc
        family-names: Blanchet
    journal: "Living Reviews in Relativity"
    volume: 17
    year: 2014
    doi: "10.12942/lrr-2014-2"
    notes: >-
      Primary source for the quadrupole formalism (eqs. 1-4) and the
      polarization decomposition (eqs. 69a-69b).

  - type: article
    title: "The basics of gravitational wave theory"
    authors:
      - given-names: Éanna É.
        family-names: Flanagan
      - given-names: Scott A.
        family-names: Hughes
    journal: "New Journal of Physics"
    volume: 7
    year: 2005
    doi: "10.1088/1367-2630/7/1/204"
    notes: >-
      Transverse-traceless projector (eq. 4.22) and geodesic deviation
      (eq. 3.11). Two verified typographical errors in this paper's worked
      binary example are recorded in docs/ERRATA.md.

  - type: article
    title: "Properties and patterns of polarized gravitational waves"
    authors:
      - given-names: Bahram
        family-names: Mashhoon
      - given-names: Sohrab
        family-names: Rahvar
    journal: "Universe"
    volume: 9
    year: 2023
    doi: "10.3390/universe9010006"
    notes: >-
      Eq. 4, the e^(2i psi) polarization rotation law. Verified 2026-08-03 and
      adopted after the previously cited equation was found to be the TT
      projector rather than the rotation law.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# preferred-citation is deliberately absent.
#
# A manuscript exists (docs/paper/nature-draft.md) but is unpublished, and
# pointing `preferred-citation` at an unpublished draft would invite citations
# to something with no DOI, no peer review and a Results section whose figures
# are still draft-quality. Add it when the paper lands, not before.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
